  "captions": {
    "README hero": "*Figure 0 (Wave-4, `F1` screen, sandbox): per-generation screen `KPI_score` across `10` seeds on repaired operators, with `72` Monte Carlo samples per candidate. Panels A and B: uniform random search (`B1`) ends with the higher best score, and `B3` leads only on population median. Panel C is a gene-label schematic, not compiled geometry; its labels are read from the receipt (`CT-35` Hinze diameter `64.0 \u00b5m`). Panel D repeats archived Wave-1/2 carrier CFD of different subjects ([`docs/results/wave2-tpms-elite-gci-rsm-and-f2-oil-v2.json`](docs/results/wave2-tpms-elite-gci-rsm-and-f2-oil-v2.json)), not Wave-4 designs, and its percentages compare with the different-pipeline `CT-35` case; against the same-pipeline control there is no detectable difference (WHITEPAPER \u00a74.6). Source: [`docs/results/wave4-evolution-proof-v1.json`](docs/results/wave4-evolution-proof-v1.json); figure `SHA-256: fab2f5f59ab5dc75cb90416dc24aaa2beafab4dee1a97888c6b783bdeb9602b0`. The pre-repair Wave-3 hero is listed with the figure plates in [`docs/RESULTS.md`](docs/RESULTS.md). Only `A1`, `A2` and `B3` start from the same generation 0; `B2` starts from conventionalized copies of those programs and `B1` from random samples. The dashed line in Panel B is an authored research-screen threshold, not a permit limit.*\n",
    "WHITEPAPER lead": "*What the figure shows, in one sentence:* with `10` seeds per arm and the same budget of `450` charged proposals per seed, evolution (`B3`) does not beat uniform random search (`B1`) on best screen score ($p_{\\text{Holm}} = 1.0$), beats it only on population median ($p_{\\text{Holm}} = 0.0012568$), and ends well above `CT-35` on a screen that rewards the genes the search activates; no held-out geometry test or physical measurement stands behind any of it ([`docs/results/wave4-evolution-proof-v1.json`](results/wave4-evolution-proof-v1.json), \u00a75.7).\n*Lead figure (Wave-4, `F1` screen, sandbox; [`docs/results/wave4-evolution-proof-v1.json`](results/wave4-evolution-proof-v1.json), generator `tools/generate_page1_figure.py`).*\n*It plots the best screen `KPI_score` found so far against generation for evolution (`B3`) and uniform random\nsearch (`B1`). Each arm has `10` seeds and `450` charged proposals per seed. Bands are 95% bootstrap intervals of the\nseed mean, thin lines are single seeds, and dotted lines are population medians. `B1` ends higher on best score\n(`45.936820` against `45.131872`; for `B3` > `B1`, $p_{\\text{Holm}} = 1.0$ and Cliff's $\\delta = -0.68$), so on\nrepaired code evolution does not beat random search on the screen's best score. `B3` leads only on population\nmedian (`43.628366` against `37.983620`). `CT-35` is a single frozen reference evaluation at `23.931038`, not a\nsearch arm. These are values of a screen built from gene-derived descriptors, not physical performance\n(correction C4). The same-pipeline CFD control shows no pressure-drop advantage for the archived shells either (\u00a74.6).*\n"
  },
